comments[7]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-13T21:41:33.254Z",codex-root,"History-completeness audit 2026-07-13: before this audit mutated the tracker, all 1,792 discovered history streams and all 28,195 events were read; integrity checks found zero missing streams, unreadable logs, hash mismatches, chain mismatches, or history drift. Item mutation integrity is therefore currently healthy. Keep this item open because workspace-scoped configuration, schema, profile, initialization, and other non-item mutation paths still need explicit immutable audit coverage and restoration semantics, coordinated with pm-klo8."
  "2026-07-24T05:17:57.275Z",claude-code-agent,"Investigation evidence: the current repository has multiple overlapping mutation inventories (CLI registration families, migration write gates, telemetry roots, MCP action contracts). A truthful contract-driven execute-every-mutation gate requires first consolidating those sources into one executable registration contract; this PR does not pretend a static scope list satisfies that acceptance criterion. Returning this item to open while the three fully implemented mutation-integrity items remain active."
  "2026-07-24T22:21:52.156Z",claude-code-agent,"Partial implementation evidence: PM_MUTATION_ACTION_CONTRACTS now provides one public typed classification of core mutation actions and required item/workspace history scopes, with drift tests and SDK documentation. This does not yet satisfy the item acceptance criterion to execute every classified mutation against a sandbox and assert op/author, so the item will return to open rather than being falsely closed."
  "2026-07-24T22:26:09.585Z",claude-code-agent,"Delivery PR: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/684"
  "2026-07-29T16:56:42.228Z","harness:claude-code","The unrecorded-workspace-operation set gained its most consequential member. Measured on a sandbox tracker at build 2026.7.29.\n\nNone of workspace snapshot create, workspace snapshot list, or workspace snapshot restore writes a history entry - not on any item stream and not on the workspace stream. The first two are read-shaped and arguably benign. The third reverts item files and history streams to a content-addressed fingerprint: two items and three entries over two streams became one item and one entry over one stream, and no entry anywhere says it happened.\n\nThat places restore in a different class from the focus and gc and merge install cases this item already carries. Those write nothing about a change they made. Restore writes nothing about entries it removed. The full defect is now tracked as pm-6l2mza; recorded here so the presence census stays complete and so a future gate over \"which operations must record\" covers the snapshot group.\n"
  "2026-07-29T21:26:22.114Z","harness:codex","Complete mutation-history census 2026-07-29: 41,391 events across 2,208 history streams. Major operations include 10,656 updates, 7,720 comments, 6,389 file links, 2,992 test links, 2,633 claims, 2,169 releases, 2,165 creates, 2,159 closes, 1,375 docs links, and audited repair/compact/redact events. Hash-chain validation is clean. The remaining acceptance boundary is coverage completeness generated from the live mutation contract, including workspace, relationship, package, merge-install, and future extension-provided mutations."
  "2026-08-11T13:53:43.890Z","harness:claude-code","Verified 2026-08-11 on the live tracker at 2026.8.11: pm focus pm-doxj appends zero history entries. _workspace.jsonl held 9 lines before and 9 after. Session state does change - .agents/pm/runtime/session.json gained focused_item plus a semantic_attribution block - so the mutation is real and unrecorded. This is the reference instance for the completeness gate this item owns: a mutating action whose effect is observable in workspace state and absent from every history stream. The same probe confirms pm config scope set DOES record config:set:<dotted.key>, so the gap is specific to session state rather than to workspace singletons generally."
notes[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T17:55:54.464Z","harness:claude-code","Live measurement of the two gate scopes, 2026-07-25, so the phased acceptance criteria can be checked against real numbers rather than an assumed inventory.\n\nItem scope (phase 1) — 30 distinct ops appear across the 2,031 per-item history streams (34,160 entries total): comment_add 7,096, update 5,992, files_add 5,948, tests_add 2,759, claim 2,429, close 1,990, create 1,989, release 1,958, docs_add 1,243, test_run_track 733, learning_add 571, note_add 563, update_audit 309, append 114, plan_complete_step 85, plan_add_step 71, plan_update_step 44, history_compact_baseline 41, history_compact 41, history_repair 35, update_ownership_bypass 29, history_redact 21, plan_create_metadata 19, plan_create_initial_step 12, plan_append_discovery 12, plan_append_validation 9, delete 9, plan_append_decision 7, plan_approve 6, plan_link_step 4.\n\nThat observed set is the ground truth the contract enumeration must cover, and it is a useful cross-check in both directions: an op that appears here but is not enumerated by the mutation tables means the tables are incomplete, and an enumerated mutating action that never appears here is either dead or untested by dogfooding (which pm-pww8ax now tracks as a capability-coverage question).\n\nWorkspace scope (phase 2) — the mechanism exists and is barely used. `.agents/pm/history/_workspace.jsonl` holds exactly 9 entries, all with op `history:author-acknowledge`. No entry records a settings, schema, profile, init, or extension-state mutation, which confirms the phase-2 boundary this item already declares: workspace-scope mutations are today entirely outside the append-only proof, not merely partially covered.\n\nTwo consequences worth carrying into the acceptance criteria. First, the known-unaudited allowlist for phase 1 can be seeded from the diff between the enumerated tables and the 30 observed ops, so it starts honest rather than empty. Second, because `_workspace.jsonl` already exists and carries a hash chain, phase 2 is a matter of routing existing mutations into it rather than designing a new stream — which makes the phase-2 gate cheaper than the phasing note implies, and worth reconsidering as part of pm-klo8 rather than after it."
  "2026-07-25T20:41:41.382Z","harness:claude-code","Unaudited-mutation inventory measured on the shipped 2026.7.25 build, so the known-unaudited list this item's phase-1 acceptance requires can be written from observation rather than assumption. Method: isolated sandbox workspace, one probe item, each mutating command run once with the item stream and the workspace stream line-counted before and after.\n\nRecords history (item stream, 14 of 16 probed): update (title), update (status), comment_add, note_add, learning_add, files_add, docs_add, tests_add, append, claim, release, restore, close, and create on the copy target.\n\nRecords nothing anywhere (0 item-stream lines, 0 workspace-stream lines):\n- pm focus <id> — mutates .agents/pm/runtime/session.json, verified by md5 diff over the whole tracker before and after. runtime/ is inside the gitignore block, so the mutation is neither recorded nor recoverable, and in a fleet the question \"which agent focused what, when\" has no answer.\n- pm gc — completes rc 0 with no entry in either stream, despite being the one maintenance verb that removes data.\n\nWorkspace stream (_workspace.jsonl) carries only two op kinds after the probe: config:set:<dotted.key> and schema:add-type. pm-klo8 shipped the stream and enumerated ten workspace-scope mutations in its acceptance (config set/unset, schema add-type/add-field/add-status/remove-*, profile apply, init re-runs, extension and package enable/disable). Only the first two are observable, so the phase-2 scope is larger than the stream's presence suggests.\n\nNot probed in the sandbox because they are package-owned and absent from a bare install: normalize and reindex both exit 2 with \"Unknown command\". Their audit status has to be measured with the owning packages installed."
  "2026-07-27T12:55:16.516Z","harness:claude-code","Unaudited-mutation inventory extended 2026-07-27. One addition, and it is the most consequential entry in the list so far.\n\nMethod: isolated sandbox workspace outside the repo with PM_GLOBAL_PATH pointed elsewhere and PM_NO_TELEMETRY set, running each workspace-scope mutation once and counting lines in the workspace history stream before and after.\n\nRecords a workspace history entry (8 entries from 7 commands):\n  pm config project set ids_token_length 6   -> config:set:ids.token_length\n  pm schema add-type Widget --folder widgets -> schema:add-type\n  pm schema add-status analyzing --role active -> schema:add-status\n  pm profile apply research                  -> profile:apply-types, profile:apply-statuses, profile:apply-fields, profile:apply-config, templates:save\n\nRecords nothing anywhere:\n  pm focus            (already on the list)\n  pm gc               (already on the list)\n  pm merge install    NEW\n\npm merge install is a different order of severity from focus and gc. It appends merge-driver rows to .gitattributes, which is a tracked file, and it registers driver commands in git config. It is the mechanism the whole multi-agent branch story depends on: with the drivers absent, a merge of .agents/pm marks files as conflicted with no conflict markers and git add silently keeps one side. So the command that installs the safety net for concurrent agents leaves no record that it ran, no record of what it wrote, and no record of who ran it. There is no way to answer, from the history, whether a workspace was fenced at the time a given merge happened.\n\nTwo smaller observations from the same run. Every workspace-stream entry carries a null message, so even the recorded ops have no human-readable statement of intent. And this repository's own _workspace.jsonl holds nine entries, all of a single op (history:author-acknowledge), which means none of the config, schema or profile mutations that shaped this workspace were recorded here either; they predate the recording."
  "2026-08-09T20:05:22.883Z","harness:claude-code","Precondition satisfied, and one concrete member for the known-unaudited list. Recorded 2026-08-09 evening from a sandbox measurement.\n\nThis item's acceptance criterion defers workspace-scope mutations until pm-klo8 lands, and instructs the test to assert the known-unaudited list explicitly until then. pm-klo8 is closed, and the capability is verified live rather than assumed: in a sandbox workspace, a scoped config write appends an entry to the workspace audit stream with the operation recorded as the dotted key that was set. The deferral in the acceptance criterion no longer applies, and the gate's scope can widen to workspace-scope mutations without waiting on anything.\n\nOne supported mutation remains with no immutable record anywhere, and it should be named explicitly rather than left to be discovered. Focus exits zero, writes only the runtime session file, and appends nothing: measured as a zero-line delta across every history stream in the workspace, before and after the invocation, with no workspace audit entry either. It is not a read — it changes which item subsequent selection and context reads treat as the session's focus, so it changes what those commands serve — and it is reachable from the documented surface. By the project's own standard, that every mutating action leaves an immutable record, it is a gap rather than an exemption.\n\nThe distinction worth encoding in the gate is between derived state and session state. The same measurement found that every item-scoped mutating verb also writes two runtime paths, the context-usage ledger and the metadata cache delta, and those are correctly out of scope for a history-completeness gate because they are recomputable from the record. The session file is not recomputable from anything; it is the only record that the choice was made. That is the test the gate should apply when deciding whether a workspace-scope write owes a history entry, and it is sharper than enumerating paths.\n\nWhether focus should record or should be reclassified as a read is a real decision and this note does not pre-empt it. What the gate must not do is stay silent: if the disposition is that focus is exempt, the exemption belongs on the known-unaudited list this item's acceptance criterion already requires, so that a newly added unaudited mutation still fails CI rather than joining an undocumented set."
